# Overview
#
# Linguists call a negation that sits on the object of a verb
# no-negation, after its `no` (`needs no npm`). The object is the noun
# that the verb acts on. A negation on the verb is not-negation (`does
# not need npm`). `needs no npm` negates the verb through its object, so
# the reader meets the noun before learning that the sentence is
# negative. The fix moves the negation onto the verb, so the negation
# comes first. The rule sits at Required, because it matches an exact
# phrase and every fix moves the negation the same way.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` matches a form of `need` or `require` before `no`, and a guard
# skips `more`, `less`, `fewer`, and `longer` after `no`. The rule flags
# the phrase instead of replacing it, because the fix depends on tense
# and person (`does not need`, `do not need`, `did not need`).
#
# False Positives
#
# Only `need` and `require` count, because `has no header` and `takes no
# arguments` read as plain idiom. A comparison word after `no` states a
# bound or a time instead (`requires no more than 2 GB`, `need no longer
# wait`), so the guard skips `more`, `less`, `fewer`, and `longer`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 5 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 5 of 5 sampled hits
# negated `need` or `require` through its object (`requires no special
# escapes`).
expr main ([needs, need, needed, requires, require, required] no ![more, less, fewer, longer])
let message "No-negation. Negate the verb, so `needs no npm` becomes `does not need npm`."
let description "Flags `need` and `require` negated through their object."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Consuming sites do not need npm."
allows "The file has no header."
allows "The command takes no arguments."
allows "The build requires no more than 2 GB."
